{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2678 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2ac09bd1017b935d3cc074e2cb3c4ab2d9ee8f608d677a871e10456279c07802" :edb/db-before-t 2677 :edb/db-after-t 2678 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535990 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:26:11.086Z" 13194139535990 true] [17592186161799 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/26f1037a6da42fdd87ef588234019d23d555dba8d9c02d5469215b2445da8e45.png)\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1,$ and $\\lambda_3=3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535990 true] [17592186161799 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1$, and $\\lambda_3=3$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535990 false] [17592186161799 1145 "Notice that we are given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda=-1$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda=3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block $J_1(3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda=3.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda=-1{:}$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block $J_2(-1).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely $J_1(-1) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(-1).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=-1.$\n\nComputing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1,$ say, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0} \\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/c59f5929bdbe3b35e42dc30266ac9ca947c3536e1c019b708a2d48170ea8222b.png)\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139535990 true] [17592186161799 1145 "Notice that we are given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda=-1$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n- $\\lambda=3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block $J_1(3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda=3$.\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-1{:}\n$$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block $J_2(-1)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chain:\n$\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0}$\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely $J_1(-1) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(-1)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chains:\n$v_{1}→^(A + I)0; v_{2}→^(A + I)0$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=-1$.\n\nComputing $A+I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -9\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+I)=2\n$$\n\nthere will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$, say, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$. So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0} \\[5pt] \\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139535990 false] [17592186161800 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/77995d8d2d70bc6eeb059663883375dbcad8820df3bafc225f854367ee122977.png)\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535990 true] [17592186161800 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=2$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535990 false] [17592186161800 1145 "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $3 \\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$ of algebraic multiplicity $3$.\n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to\n\n$$\n\\lambda=2{:}\n$$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $3 \\times 3$ Jordan block $J_3(2)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ corresponding to $\\lambda=2$ form the following chain:\n$\\mathbf{v}_3 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{0}$\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of one $2 \\times 2$ and one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block, say $J_2(2) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(2)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ corresponding to $\\lambda=2$ form the following chains:\n$v_{2}→^(A - 2 I)v_{1}→^(A - 2 I)0; v_{3}→^(A - 2 I)0$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=2$.\n\nComputing $A-2I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 2I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 