 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139535990 true] [17592186162219 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535990 true] [17592186162219 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535990 true] [17592186162219 1100 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = λ_{2} = λ_{3} =-3$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535990 false] [17592186162219 1145 "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $3\\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =-3:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $3\\times 3$ Jordan block ${J}_{3}(-3).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $