orm using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+5I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(-4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-5)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378450 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378451 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378452 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378453 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378454 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1041 "selection" 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378450 13194139535990 true] [17592186378449 1110 #uuid "8fa5d822-e4a2-53d3-88df-6da44baba9f4" 13194139535990 true] [17592186378450 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535990 true] [17592186378450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535990 true] [17592186378450 1111 #uui